      "text": "Let's denote the given inequality as follows:\n\\[\n\\sin(x + y) \\leq \\sin(x) + \\sin(y)\n\\]\nTo solve this inequality for all \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in the interval \\([0, \\pi]\\), we will consider the properties of the sine function and use trigonometric identities where applicable.\n\nFirst, let's express \\(\\sin(x + y)\\) in terms of \\(\\sin(x)\\) and \\(\\sin(y)\\). Recall the sine addition formula:\n\\[\n\\sin(x + y) = \\sin(x)\\cos(y) + \\cos(x)\\sin(y)\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this into the inequality, we get:\n\\[\n\\sin(x)\\cos(y) + \\cos(x)\\sin(y) \\leq \\sin(x) + \\sin(y)\n\\]\n\nNext, we will isolate \\(\\sin(x)\\cos(y) + \\cos(x)\\sin(y)\\):\n\\[\n\\sin(x)\\cos(y) + \\cos(x)\\sin(y) - \\sin(y) - \\sin(x) \\leq 0\n\\]\n\nGrouping terms to simplify, we get:\n\\[\n\\sin(x)\\cos(y) - \\sin(x) + \\cos(x)\\sin(y) - \\sin(y) \\leq 0\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(\\sin(x)\\) from the first group and \\(\\sin(y)\\) from the second group:\n\\[\n\\sin(x)(\\cos(y) - 1) + \\sin(y)(\\cos(x) - 1) \\leq 0\n\\]\n\nSince this inequality must hold for all \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in \\([0, \\pi]\\), we need to consider the behavior of both \\(\\sin(x)\\) and \\(\\sin(y)\\) over the interval. Note that \\(\\sin(x)\\) and \\(\\sin(y)\\) take values between -1 and 1.\n\nTo ensure the inequality holds universally, let's analyze specific cases:\n1. When \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = 0\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sin(0 + 0) \\leq \\sin(0) + \\sin(0) \\implies 0 \\leq 0\n   \\]\n   This is true.\n\n2. When \\(x = \\pi\\) and \\(y = \\pi\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sin(\\pi + \\pi) \\leq \\sin(\\pi) + \\sin(\\pi) \\implies \\sin(2\\pi) \\leq 0 + 0 \\implies 0 \\leq 0\n   \\]\n   This is also true.\n\n3. For general \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in \\([0, \\pi]\\), observe that the expression \\(\\sin(x + y) - \\sin(x) - \\sin(y)\\) must be non-positive. Given the period and symmetry properties of the sine function, the most restrictive condition is likely to be when \\(\\sin(x)\\) and \\(\\sin(y)\\) are close or equal.\n\nTo generalize, consider the case when \\(\\sin(x) = \\sin(y)\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sin(x)\\cos(x) + \\sin(x)\\sin(x) \\leq \\sin(x) + \\sin(x)\n   \\]\n   Simplifying further:\n   \\[\n   \\sin(x)(\\cos(x) + \\sin(x)) \\leq 2\\sin(x)\n   \\]\n\nFor the inequality to hold, \\(\\cos(x) + \\sin(x)\\) must be within the range where the product \\(\\sin(x) \\cos(x)\\) is non-positive. This implies \\(\\cos(x) \\leq 0\\) or \\(\\sin(x) \\leq 0\\). \n\nTesting specific values or critical points, we find that \\(\\sin(x) + \\sin(y) \\geq \\sin(x)\\cos(y) + \\cos(x)\\sin(y)\\) holds in \\([0, \\pi]\\) when \\(\\sin(x) \\leq 0\\) or \\(\\sin(y) \\leq 0\\). \n\nThus, the final answer is that the inequality holds for all \\( y \\in [0, \\pi] \\) under the condition that \\( \\sin(x) \\leq 0 \\) or \\( \\sin(y) \\leq 0 \\). Since \\(\\sin(x) \\leq 0\\) or \\(\\sin(y) \\leq 0\\) when neither is positive, it holds for:\n\\[\n\\boxed{[0, \\pi]}\n\\]",
