      "text": "Let's start with the given inequality:\n\\[\n\\sin(x+y) \\leq \\sin(x) + \\sin(y)\n\\]\nfor all \\( x \\) in the interval \\( [0, \\pi] \\).\n\nFirst, recall the angle addition formula for sine:\n\\[\n\\sin(x+y) = \\sin x \\cos y + \\cos x \\sin y.\n\\]\nThus, the inequality becomes:\n\\[\n\\sin x \\cos y + \\cos x \\sin y \\leq \\sin x + \\sin y.\n\\]\n\nRearrange the terms:\n\\[\n\\sin x \\cos y + \\cos x \\sin y - \\sin x - \\sin y \\leq 0.\n\\]\n\nFactor the left-hand side:\n\\[\n\\sin x (\\cos y - 1) + \\sin y (\\cos x - 1) \\leq 0.\n\\]\n\nIn order for this inequality to hold for all \\( x \\) in the interval \\( [0, \\pi] \\), we need to consider the behavior of the functions involved. The term \\( \\sin x \\) ranges from 0 to 1 on this interval. We will analyze the inequality for different cases based on the value of \\( \\sin x \\).\n\n### Case 1: \\( \\sin x = 0 \\)\nIf \\( \\sin x = 0 \\), then \\( \\sin y (\\cos x - 1) \\leq 0 \\). Since \\( \\cos x = 1 \\) when \\( x = 0 \\) and \\( \\cos x = 0 \\) when \\( x = \\pi \\), we have:\n- When \\( x = 0 \\), \\( \\sin x = 0 \\) and \\( \\sin y (\\cos x - 1) = -\\sin y \\leq 0 \\), so \\( \\sin y \\geq 0 \\) (since \\(\\sin y = 0\\) or \\(\\sin y \\geq 1\\) is not possible for \\( x = 0 \\)).\n- When \\( x = \\pi \\), \\( \\sin x = 0 \\), and \\( \\sin y (\\cos x - 1) = \\sin y \\leq 0 \\), so \\( \\sin y \\leq 0 \\) (since \\(\\sin y = 0\\) or \\(\\sin y \\leq -1\\) is not possible).\n\nThus, when \\( \\sin x = 0 \\), the inequality holds for all \\( \\sin y \\in [0, 1] \\).\n\n### Case 2: \\( \\sin x \\geq 0 \\)\nFor \\( \\sin x \\geq 0 \\), the inequality becomes:\n\\[\n\\sin x \\cos y + \\sin y (\\cos x - 1) \\leq 0.\n\\]\nSince \\( \\sin x \\geq 0 \\), the term \\( \\sin x (\\cos y - 1) \\) must be non-positive. For this to hold for all \\( \\cos y \\in [0, 1] \\), we need to ensure that \\( \\cos y \\geq \\cos x \\). When \\( x = 0 \\), \\( \\cos x = 1 \\), and when \\( x = \\pi \\), \\( \\cos x = -1 \\). Therefore, we need:\n\\[\n\\cos y \\geq 1.\n\\]\nSince \\( \\cos y \\) cannot be greater than or equal to 1 within the interval \\( [0, \\pi] \\), this case is not possible.\n\n### Case 3: \\( \\sin x < 0 \\)\nFor \\( \\sin x < 0 \\), the inequality becomes:\n\\[\n\\sin x \\cos y + \\sin y (\\cos x - 1) \\leq 0.\n\\]\nSince \\( \\sin x < 0 \\), the term \\( \\sin x (\\cos y - 1) \\) must be non-positive. For this to hold for all \\( \\cos y \\in [0, 1] \\), we need to ensure that \\( \\cos y \\leq \\cos x \\). Since \\( \\cos y \\) ranges from 0 to 1, and \\( \\cos x \\) ranges from -1 to 1, the inequality holds for all \\( \\cos y \\in [0, 1] \\).\n\nThus, the inequality \\( \\cos y \\leq \\cos x \\) must hold for all \\( x \\in [0, \\pi] \\). This ensures that the original inequality holds for all \\( x \\in [0, \\pi] \\).\n\n### Conclusion\nThe inequality \\( \\sin(x+y) \\leq \\sin(x) + \\sin(y) \\) holds for all \\( x \\in [0, \\pi] \\) if and only if \\( \\cos y \\leq \\cos x \\) for all \\( x \\in [0, \\pi] \\). Since this condition is always satisfied for \\( y \\in [0, \\pi] \\), the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{[0, \\pi]}\n\\]",
